{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KoxaDLoP43ajnRheVt5FcR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Read-only architecture task\n2\t\n3\tPlan the implementation of the securitized-product trade capture mock described\n4\tin `benchmark/projects/T7-securitized-trade-capture/PRODUCT.md`.\n5\t\n6\tInspect the existing application structure, package scripts, TypeScript setup,\n7\tand test approach before proposing files. This is a read-only task. Do not edit,\n8\tcreate, rename, or delete files. Do not use the network, install dependencies,\n9\tchange Git state, start a service, or inspect `benchmark/private` or\n10\t`benchmark/runs`.\n11\t\n12\tThe implementation must be divided across four bounded workers:\n13\t\n14\t1. Codex + GPT-5.6 Terra for domain types, calculations, validation, seed data,\n15\t   and domain tests.\n16\t2. Claude Code + Sonnet 5 for the accessible trade-ticket component.\n17\t3. OpenCode + Qwen3.8-27B for a small desk-insights component.\n18\t4. OpenCode + Kimi K3 for application state, blotter, selected-trade review,\n19\t   and visual system.\n20\t\n21\tCodex + GPT-5.6 Sol will integrate. Claude Code + Opus 5 will review the plan\n22\tand later review the completed application.\n23\t\n24\tReturn a concise report with these exact sections:\n25\t\n26\t## Product interpretation\n27\t\n28\tState the workflow and the most important simplifying assumptions.\n29\t\n30\t## Architecture\n31\t\n32\tDefine the route, client/server boundary, state ownership, domain module, and\n33\tcomponent tree.\n34\t\n35\t## Stable contracts\n36\t\n37\tSpecify exact exported TypeScript types, functions, and component props that\n38\tmust be frozen before worker execution.\n39\t\n40\t## File ownership\n41\t\n42\tGive each of the four implementation workers an explicit, non-overlapping list\n43\tof owned files. Identify the small set of integrator-owned files.\n44\t\n45\t## Worker tasks\n46\t\n47\tFor each worker, give the acceptance requirements, commands it may run, and\n48\twhat it must not change.\n49\t\n50\t## Integration sequence\n51\t\n52\tExplain how Sol should combine the four patches and how to handle an interface\n53\tmismatch without hiding a worker failure.\n54\t\n55\t## Verification\n56\t\n57\tDefine domain tests, type checking, lint, build, rendered-HTML assertions, and\n58\tmanual checks. Separate checks that can run in isolated worker branches from the\n59\tfinal integrated gate.\n60\t\n61\t## Risks and review focus\n62\t\n63\tList the five most important domain or engineering risks for the Opus reviewer.\n64\t\n65\tKeep the report below 1,800 words. Cite concrete repository paths for claims\n66\tabout the existing application.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64ac4aac-c683-4d8a-a5fd-3c35d9ba90ee","uuid":"bfcf0fcb-854f-45f8-b685-ae8f4b760a91","timestamp":"2026-08-23T22:05:02.029Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/cs/y_h_9x9j2hj316xhs3mvszd80000gn/T/coding-assistant-benchmark-40yaLF/checkout/benchmark/tasks/T7-trade-capture-plan/prompt.md","content":"# Read-only architecture task\n\nPlan the implementation of the securitized-product trade capture mock described\nin `benchmark/projects/T7-securitized-trade-capture/PRODUCT.md`.\n\nInspect the existing application structure, package scripts, TypeScript setup,\nand test approach before proposing files. This is a read-only task. Do not edit,\ncreate, rename, or delete files. Do not use the network, install dependencies,\nchange Git state, start a service, or inspect `benchmark/private` or\n`benchmark/runs`.\n\nThe implementation must be divided across four bounded workers:\n\n1. Codex + GPT-5.6 Terra for domain types, calculations, validation, seed data,\n   and domain tests.\n2. Claude Code + Sonnet 5 for the accessible trade-ticket component.\n3. OpenCode + Qwen3.8-27B for a small desk-insights component.\n4. OpenCode + Kimi K3 for application state, blotter, selected-trade review,\n   and visual system.\n\nCodex + GPT-5.6 Sol will integrate. Claude Code + Opus 5 will review the plan\nand later review the completed application.\n\nReturn a concise report with these exact sections:\n\n## Product interpretation\n\nState the workflow and the most important simplifying assumptions.\n\n## Architecture\n\nDefine the route, client/server boundary, state ownership, domain module, and\ncomponent tree.\n\n## Stable contracts\n\nSpecify exact exported TypeScript types, functions, and component props that\nmust be frozen before worker execution.\n\n## File ownership\n\nGive each of the four implementation workers an explicit, non-overlapping list\nof owned files. Identify the small set of integrator-owned files.\n\n## Worker tasks\n\nFor each worker, give the acceptance requirements, commands it may run, and\nwhat it must not change.\n\n## Integration sequence\n\nExplain how Sol should combine the four patches and how to handle an interface\nmismatch without hiding a worker failure.\n\n## Verification\n\nDefine domain tests, type checking, lint, build, rendered-HTML assertions, and\nmanual checks. Separate checks that can run in isolated worker branches from the\nfinal integrated gate.\n\n## Risks and review focus\n\nList the five most important domain or engineering risks for the Opus reviewer.\n\nKeep the report below 1,800 words. Cite concrete repository paths for claims\nabout the existing application.\n","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
